Reading `INTENT.md` and all source files reveals multiple violations of clauses P1–P6 and several production defects.

**P1 (checksum)** — `src/checksum.rs:5` initializes the CRC to `0x0000`; the intent mandates `0xFFFF`. This breaks the interoperability contract (K3).

**P2 (bounds)** — `src/frame.rs:11` sets `MAX_PAYLOAD = 65536`, allowing a 65536-byte payload when the intent caps it at 65535.

**P4 (malformed frames)** — `src/parse.rs:51` returns `Ok(None)` on checksum mismatch instead of `FrameError::Corrupt`, silently dropping the frame. Additionally, `src/parse.rs:68` swallows all `FrameError` values inside `drain`, so malformed frames never surface to the caller.

**P5 (arithmetic)** — `src/buffer.rs:27` performs `self.available() + chunk.len()` without overflow checking, so a malicious chunk can wrap the sum and bypass `HIGH_WATER`. In `src/parse.rs:65`, `drain` decrements `budget -= consumed` without checking underflow; when the handler reentrantly feeds more bytes (K2), the next frame can be larger than the remaining budget, causing a panic in debug or wrap in release.

**Missing bounds check** — `src/parse.rs:44` slices `data[HEADER_LEN..HEADER_LEN + len]` (and line 46 reads the trailer) without first verifying `data.len() >= frame_len`. A valid header with an incomplete frame causes a panic instead of returning `Ok(None)`.

